---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-and-erectile-dysfunction`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D and Erectile Dysfunction',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  about: [{ '@type': 'MedicalCondition', name: 'Erectile dysfunction', code: { '@type': 'MedicalCode', codeValue: 'N52', codingSystem: 'ICD-10' } }],
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D and ED', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D and Erectile Dysfunction: Endothelial Function, Testosterone, Evidence"
  description="Vitamin D and ED — the endothelial dysfunction and testosterone mechanisms, observational data, small supplementation trials, and reasonable practical approach."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-and-erectile-dysfunction", label: "Vitamin D and ED" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D and Erectile Dysfunction</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Erectile dysfunction (ED) is largely a vascular disease — endothelial
      dysfunction driven by the same processes that cause coronary and peripheral
      atherosclerosis. Vitamin D deficiency is a modifiable factor in endothelial
      dysfunction; correcting it may modestly improve ED alongside evidence-based
      management.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Observational data</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Adults with ED have lower mean 25(OH)D than age-matched controls in multiple
      studies. NHANES data (Farag 2016) found deficient men had 32% higher odds of
      ED. The Bologna cohort found deficient men had more severe ED and worse
      penile Doppler flow measures.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Mechanisms</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Endothelial function</strong> — vitamin D deficiency associates with reduced flow-mediated dilation and increased oxidative stress; correction can improve endothelial biomarkers.</li>
      <li><strong>Testosterone</strong> — vitamin D-deficient men may have modestly lower testosterone; correction sometimes helps in the truly deficient (see <a href="/vitamin-d-and-testosterone" class="text-amber-700 underline">testosterone page</a>).</li>
      <li><strong>Cardiovascular risk factor overlap</strong> — hypertension, diabetes, dyslipidaemia, and obesity all cluster with vitamin D deficiency and drive ED.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Supplementation trial evidence</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Small RCTs (Canguven 2017) reported IIEF-5 (International Index of Erectile
      Function) improvement with vitamin D correction in deficient men. Sample
      sizes are small and blinding was limited. No large trial has established
      vitamin D as a first-line ED treatment.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What actually works for ED</h2>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li>PDE5 inhibitors (sildenafil, tadalafil, vardenafil, avanafil) — first-line pharmacotherapy.</li>
      <li>Cardiovascular risk factor management — BP, lipids, glycaemic control, smoking cessation.</li>
      <li>Weight loss and regular aerobic exercise — meaningful improvements in mild-moderate ED.</li>
      <li>Address depression and anxiety.</li>
      <li>Vacuum devices, intracavernosal or intraurethral prostaglandin, or penile implant for refractory ED.</li>
      <li>Vitamin D correction is a low-cost adjunct, not a stand-alone treatment.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">ED as cardiovascular warning</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      New-onset ED in middle-aged men should prompt cardiovascular risk assessment
      — endothelial dysfunction in penile arteries typically precedes coronary
      artery disease by 3–5 years. This is a valuable early-warning signal that
      matters more than any vitamin question.
    </p>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> ED warrants medical evaluation — both
      to identify underlying cardiovascular risk and to access effective treatment.
      Do not skip evaluation in favour of supplements.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-and-erectile-dysfunction" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
